![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

When calculating file hashes, wrap the operations in a defensive `try-catch` block to handle unreadable files or I/O failures gracefully, returning `null` to preserve fail-closed behavior.

```javascript
function sha256File(root, relPath) {
  const target = resolvePath(root, relPath);
  if (!target || !fs.existsSync(target)) return null;
  try {
    return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(fs.readFileSync(target)).digest('hex');
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When reading files or calculating file hashes, wrap the operations in a defensive try-catch block to handle unreadable files or I/O failures gracefully, returning null to preserve fail-closed behavior.
</details>

